"A note on local antimagic chromatic number of lexicographic product graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.14707)\nciteturn7academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.2 years ago; Title: A note on local antimagic chromatic number of lexicographic product graphs ... We conjecture that (i) there are infinitely many graphs $G$ and $H$ such that $\\chi_{la}(G[H])=\\chi_{la}(G)\\chi_{la}(H) = \\chi(G)\\chi(H)$, and (ii) for $k\\ge 1$, $\\chi_{la}(G[H]) = \\chi(G)\\chi(H)$ if and only if $\\chi(G)\\chi(H) = 2\\chi(H) + \\lceil\\frac{\\chi(H)}{k}\\rceil$, where $2k+1$ is the length of a shortest odd cycle in $G$.\nTitle: A note on local antimagic chromatic number of lexicographic product graphs\nAuthors: Gee-Choon Lau, Wai-Chee Shiu, K. Premalatha, Ruixue Zhang, M. Nalliah\nDate: Wed Aug 31 09:13:32 2022\n\nLet $G = (V,E)$ be a connected simple graph. A bijection $f: E \\rightarrow \\{1,2,\\ldots,|E|\\}$ is called a local antimagic labeling of $G$ if $f^+(u) \\neq f^+(v)$ holds for any two adjacent vertices $u$ and $v$, where $f^+(u) = \\sum_{e\\in E(u)} f(e)$ and $E(u$) is the set of edges incident to $u$. A graph $G$ is called local antimagic if $G$ admits at least a local antimagic labeling. The local antimagic chromatic number, denoted $\\chi_{la}(G)$, is the minimum number of induced colors taken over local antimagic labelings of $G$. Let $G$ and $H$ be two disjoint graphs. The graph $G[H]$ is obtained by the lexicographic product of $G$ and $H$. In this paper, we obtain sufficient conditions for $\\chi_{la}(G[H])\\leq \\chi_{la}(G)\\chi_{la}(H)$. Consequently, we give examples of $G$ and $H$ such that $\\chi_{la}(G[H]) = \\chi(G)\\chi(H)$, where $\\chi(G)$ is the chromatic number of $G$. We conjecture that (i) there are infinitely many graphs $G$ and $H$ such that $\\chi_{la}(G[H])=\\chi_{la}(G)\\chi_{la}(H) = \\chi(G)\\chi(H)$, and (ii) for $k\\ge 1$, $\\chi_{la}(G[H]) = \\chi(G)\\chi(H)$ if and only if $\\chi(G)\\chi(H) = 2\\chi(H) + \\lceil\\frac{\\chi(H)}{k}\\rceil$, where $2k+1$ is the length of a shortest odd cycle in $G$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLocal total anti-magic chromatic number of graphs - PMC (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10344762/)\nciteturn7search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.3 years ago; Crawled: last week; A bijection $\\pi :V(G)\\cup E(G)\\to \\{1,2,....,n+m\\}$ is said to be local total anti-magic labeling of a graph G if it satisfies the conditions: (i.) for any edge uv, $\\omega (u)\\ne \\omega (v)$, where u and v in $V(G)$ (ii.) for any two adjacent edges e and ${e}^{\\prime }$, $\\omega (e)\\ne \\omega ({e}^{\\prime })$ (iii.) for any edge $uv\\in E(G)$ is incident to the vertex v, $\\omega (v)\\ne \\omega (uv)$, where weight of vertex u is, $\\omega (u)={\\sum }_{e\\in S(u)}\\pi (e)$, $S(u)$ is the set of edges with every edge of $S(u)$ one end vertex is u and an edge weight is $\\omega (e=uv)=\\pi (u)+\\pi (v)$. In this paper, we have introduced a local total anti-magic labeling (LTAL) and the local total anti-magic chromatic number (LTACN). ... > ### Conjecture 1.2 [9] —\n\n. 2023 Jul 4;9(7):e17761. doi: 10.1016/j.heliyon.2023.e17761\n\n# Local total anti-magic chromatic number of graphs\n\nReceived 2023 Feb 13; Revised 2023 Jun 24; Accepted 2023 Jun 27; Collection date 2023 Jul.\n\nPMCID: PMC10344762  PMID: 37456059\n\n## Abstract\n\nLet $G=(V,E)$ be a graph without isolated vertices and let $|V(G)|=n$ and $|E(G)|=m$. A bijection $\\pi :V(G)\\cup E(G)\\to \\{1,2,....,n+m\\}$ is said to be local total anti-magic labeling of a graph G if it satisfies the conditions: (i.) for any edge uv, $\\omega (u)\\ne \\omega (v)$, where u and v in $V(G)$ (ii.) for any two adjacent edges e and ${e}^{\\prime }$, $\\omega (e)\\ne \\omega ({e}^{\\prime })$ (iii.) for any edge $uv\\in E(G)$ is incident to the vertex v, $\\omega (v)\\ne \\omega (uv)$, where weight of vertex u is, $\\omega (u)={\\sum }_{e\\in S(u)}\\pi (e)$, $S(u)$ is the set of edges with every edge of $S(u)$ one end vertex is u and an edge weight is $\\omega (e=uv)=\\pi (u)+\\pi (v)$. In this paper, we have introduced a local total anti-magic labeling (LTAL) and the local total anti-magic chromatic number (LTACN). The idea of an anti-magic labeling of a graph were proposed by Hartsfield and Ringel [9].\n\n> ### Definition 1.2 [9] —\n> \n> A bijection $\\pi :E\\to \\{1,2,3,...,m\\}$ is called an anti-magic labeling of G, if $\\omega (u)\\ne \\omega (v)$ for any two distinct vertices u, $v\\in V(G)$ and the weight of the vertex u is $\\omega (u)={\\sum }_{e\\in S(u)}\\pi (e)$, where $S(u)$ is the set of edges with every edge of $S(u)$ one end vertex is u. A graph G is an anti-magic if G has an anti-magic labeling.\n\nHartsfield and Ringel [9] proposed the following conjectures.\n\n> ### Conjecture 1.1 [9] —\n> \n> Every connected graph with except${P}_{2}$is anti-magic.\n\n> ### Conjecture 1.2 [9] —\n> \n> Every tree with except${P}_{2}$is anti-magic.\n\nSeveral authors are studied the anti-magic labeling for several families of graphs. Bača et al. [3] obtained the anti-magic labelings for some graphs in the family of join graphs. Chang et al. [5] proved that all even degree regular graphs are anti-magic. For further reference see in [19], [18], [22].\n\nIn 2017, Arumugam et al. [1] proposed the local anti-magic chromatic number using anti-magic labeling and vertex coloring concepts. The formal definition is given as follows.\n\n> ### Definition 1.3 [1] —\n> \n> A local anti-magic labeling is a bijection $\\pi :E\\to \\{1,2,....,m\\}$ such that $w(u)\\ne w(v)$, for all $uv\\in E$, where $w(u)={\\sum }_{e\\in S(u)}\\pi (e)$, and $S(u)$ is the set of edges with every edge of $S(u)$ one end vertex is u. If a graph G has local anti-magic labeling, then G is local anti-magic. The least number of colors that required to produce a local anti-magic labeling of G is called local anti-magic chromatic number.\n\nThe local anti-magic chromatic number of a graph G is denoted by ${\\chi }_{la}(G)$. They obtained the following results and proposed the following conjectures.\n\n> ### Theorem 1.3 [1] —\n> \n> For any tree T with k leaves,${\\chi }_{la}(T)\\ge k+1$.\n\n> ### Theorem 1.4 [1] —\n> \n> Let${P}_{n},n>2$be the path graph. Then${\\chi }_{la}({P}_{n})=3$.\n\n> ### Theorem 1.5 [1] —\n> \n> Let${C}_{n}$be the cycle graph. Then${\\chi }_{la}({C}_{n})=3$.\n\n> ### Theorem 1.6 [1] —\n> \n> For the friendship graph${F}_{n}$, we have${\\chi }_{la}({F}_{n})=3$.\n\n> ### Conjecture 1.3 [1] —\n> \n> Every connected graph with except${K}_{2}$is local anti-magic.\n\n> ### Conjecture 1.4 [1] —\n> \n> Every tree with except${K}_{2}$is local anti-magic.\n\nPremalatha et al. [15] obtained the local anti-magic chromatic number for several families of trees. Nalliah et al. [13] found the local anti-magic chromatic number for generalized friendship graph of complete graphs and cycles. \n> ### Theorem 2.8\n> \n> Let${S}_{n,n},n>5$be the double star graph. Then${\\chi }_{lt}({S}_{n,n})=2n+2$.\n\n> ### Proof\n> \n> Let $V({S}_{n,n})=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{u}_{i},{v}_{j},1\\le i\\le n,1\\le j\\le n\\}$ and $E({S}_{n,n})=\\{{x}_{1}{x}_{2},{x}_{1}{u}_{i},{x}_{2}{v}_{j},1\\le i\\le n,1\\le j\\le n\\}$, be the vertex and edge sets of ${S}_{n,n}$. Then $|V({S}_{n,n})|=p=2n+2$, $|E({S}_{n,n})|=q=2n+1$ and $|V|+|E|=p+q=4n+3$.\n> \n> Suppose ${S}_{n,n}$ admits a LTA labeling f. Then there exists a bijection $f:V\\cup E\\to \\{1,2,3,...,4n+3\\}$. By Theorem 2.1, we get ${\\chi }_{lt}({S}_{n,n})\\ge 2n+1$. Suppose ${\\chi }_{lt}({S}_{n,n})=2n+1$. Then there exists a LTA labeling f of G with $2n+1$ colors. Now, we consider the following cases:\n> \n> Case 1:$f({x}_{1})=p+q$ or $f({u}_{1})=p+q$\n> \n> If $f({x}_{1})=p+q$, then its incident edges $e={x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ and ${e}^{\\prime }={x}_{1}{u}_{1}$ weights are more than $p+q$. Since e and ${e}^{\\prime }$ are adjacent, it follows, we get ${\\chi }_{lt}({S}_{n,n})\\ge 2n+2$. Similarly, we get ${\\chi }_{lt}({S}_{n,n})\\ge 2n+2$ if $f({x}_{2})=p+q$. If $f({u}_{1})=p+q$ or $f({v}_{1})=p+q$, then $w(e={u}_{1}{x}_{1})>p+q$, an edge $e={u}_{1}{x}_{1}$ received a color ${c}_{2n+1}$, and hence all other vertex weights are less than $p+q$. If $w({x}_{1})<p+q=4n+3$, the weight of the vertex ${x}_{1}$ is $w({x}_{1})\\ge \\frac{(n+1)(n+2)}{2}$. The weight of the vertex ${x}_{1}$, $w({x}_{1})$ is not more than $4n+3$, that is $\\frac{(n+1)(n+2)}{2}\\le 4n+3$, which implies, we get $n(n-5)-4>0$ if $n\\ge 6$, which is a contradiction. Thus, the vertex ${x}_{1}$ received a new color ${c}_{2n+2}$. Hence ${\\chi }_{lt}({S}_{n,n})\\ge 2n+2$.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBulletin of the ICA (https://pages.mtu.edu/~kreher/BICA/Abstracts/BICA2024-24-Abstract.pdf)\nciteturn7search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; Haslegrave, Proof of a local antimagic conjecture, *Discrete Math. ... Shiu, On local antimagic chromatic number of cycle related join graphs II, *Discrete Math.\nBulletin of the ICA  \nArticle in press.\n\n# Construction of local antimagic 3-colorable graphs of fixed even size: Matrix approach\n\nGee-Choon Lau, Wai Chee Shiu, M. Nalliah, and K. Premalatha\n\n**Abstract.** An edge labeling of a connected graph \\(G=(V,E)\\) containing \\(m\\) edges is said to be local antimagic if it is a bijection \\(f:E \\to \\{1,\\ldots,m\\}\\) such that, for any pair of adjacent vertices \\(u\\) and \\(v\\), we have \\(f^+(u) \\ne f^+(v)\\), where the induced vertex label \\(f^+(u)=\\sum f(e)\\) with \\(e\\) ranging over all the edges incident with \\(u\\). The local antimagic chromatic number of \\(G\\), denoted by \\(\\chi_{la}(G)\\), is the minimum number of distinct induced vertex labels over all local antimagic labelings of \\(G\\). In this paper, we give ways to construct matrices with integers in \\([1,10k]\\), \\(k \\ge 1\\), that meet certain properties. Consequently, we obtain many families of (disconnected) bipartite (and tripartite) graphs of size \\(10k\\) with local antimagic chromatic number 3.\n\n## References\n\n[1] S. Arumugam, K. Premalatha, M. Bača, and A. Semaničová-Fenovčíková, Local antimagic vertex coloring of a graph, *Graphs Combin.* **33** (2017), 275–285.\n\n[2] J. Haslegrave, Proof of a local antimagic conjecture, *Discrete Math. Theor. Comput. Sci.* **20**(1) (2018), Paper No. 18, 14 pp.\n\n[3] G. C. Lau, H. K. Ng, and W. C. Shiu, Affirmative solutions on local antimagic chromatic number, *Graphs Combin.* **36** (2020), 1337–1354.\n\n[4] G. C. Lau, K. Premalatha, S. Arumugam, and W. C. Shiu, On local antimagic chromatic number of cycle related join graphs II, *Discrete Math. Algorithms Appl.* (2023), 2350022 (18 pages).\n\n**Key words and phrases:** Local antimagic 3-colorable graph, local antimagic chromatic number, matrix, even size  \n**Mathematics Subject Classifications:** 05C78, 05C69  \n**Corresponding author:** Gee-Choon Lau <geec lau@yahoo.com>\n\nReceived: 3 May 2024  \nAccepted: 24 April 2025\n\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n| ICA --- | ---ICA BULLETIN OF THE ICA (https://bica.the-ica.org/Volumes/107/Reprints/BICA2024-24-Reprint.pdf)\nciteturn7search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Haslegrave, Proof of a local antimagic conjecture, Discrete Math. ... antimagic chromatic number of cycle related join graphs II, Discrete ... Shiu, On local antimagic chromatic number of lex-\nLau, Shiu, Nalliah, and Premalatha\nProblem 5.4. For k ≥1, determine χla(kB8).\nProblem 5.5. For k ≥1, determine χla(kC8(P3)).\nProblem 5.6. For k ≥1, determine χla(kA8).\nProblem 5.7. For rs ≥3 and rs is odd, determine χla(H(r, s)).\nThe Cartesian product of two graphs G and H is denoted by G × H. Now,\nconsider the plane graph Cp × P2, where p ≥2. We now define graph(s)\nCq(p, 2) obtained from Cp × P2 by deleting q edges not in Cp and each pair\nof these q edges are not incident with the same face, where 1 ≤q ≤p\n2. Note\nthat Cq(p, 2) is unique up to isomorphism if and only if p = 2q or p = 2q+1\nor q = 1.\nProblem 5.8. For (p, q) ̸= (8, 4), determine χla(Cq(p, 2)).\nAcknowledgment\nThe authors are grateful to the anonymous referee for the many comments\nthat improved this manuscript a great deal.\nReferences\n[1] S. Arumugam, K. Premalatha, M. Baˇca, and A. Semaniˇcov´a-\nFeˇnovˇc´ıkov´a, Local antimagic vertex coloring of a graph, Graphs Com-\nbin. 33 (2017), 275–285.\n[2] J. Haslegrave, Proof of a local antimagic conjecture, Discrete Math.\nTheor. Comput. Sci. 20(1) (2018), Paper No. 18, 14 pp.\n[3] G. C. Lau, H. K. Ng, and W. C. Shiu, Affirmative solutions on local\nantimagic chromatic number, Graphs Combin. 36 (2020), 1337–1354.\n[4] G. C. Lau, K. Premalatha, S. Arumugam, and W. C. Shiu, On local\nantimagic chromatic number of cycle related join graphs II, Discrete\nMath. Algorithms Appl. (2023), 2350022 (18 pages).\n[5] G. C. Lau and W. C. Shiu, On local antimagic chromatic number of lex-\nicographic product graphs. Acta Math. Hungar. 169 (2023), 158–170.\n[6] G. C. Lau, W. C. Shiu, and H. K. Ng, On local antimagic chromatic\nnumber of cycle-related join graphs, Discuss. Math. Graph Theory 4(1)\n(2021), 133–152, http://dx.doi.org/10.7151/dmgt.2177.\n88--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLocal Antimagic Chromatic Number for Copies of Graphs (https://www.mdpi.com/2227-7390/9/11/1230)\nciteturn7search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.4 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; In this paper, we estimate the bounds of the local antimagic chromatic number for disjoint union of multiple copies of a graph. ... Bensmail et al. [15] propose the slightly stronger form of the previous conjecture that every graph without component isomorphic to {K}_{2} has a local antimagic labeling. ... The upper bound is based on the fact that there exists a local antimagic labeling of m{P}_{3} that induces 2m+1 colors such that the color of every vertex of degree 2 in m{P}_{3} will have the same color 2m+1.\n\nIn [14] authors conjectured that any connected graph other than {K}_{2} admits a local antimagic labeling. Bensmail et al. [15] propose the slightly stronger form of the previous conjecture that every graph without component isomorphic to {K}_{2} has a local antimagic labeling. This conjecture was proved by Haslegrave [16] using the probabilistic method.\n\nAny local antimagic labeling induces a proper vertex coloring of G where the vertex-weight wt(u) is the color of u. This naturally leads to the concept of a local antimagic chromatic number introduced in [14]. The local antimagic chromatic number {\\chi }_{la}(G) is defined to be the minimum number of colors taken over all colorings of G induced by local antimagic labelings of G.\n\nFor any graph G, {\\chi }_{la}(G)\\ge \\chi (G), where \\chi (G) is the chromatic number of G as the minimum number of colors needed to produce a proper coloring of G. In [14] is investigated the local antimagic chromatic number for paths, cycles, friendship graphs, wheels and complete bipartite graphs. Moreover, there is proved that for any tree T with l leaves {\\chi }_{la}(T)\\ge l+1.\n\nIn this paper, we investigate the local antimagic chromatic number for disjoint union of multiple copies of a graph G, denoted by mG, m\\ge 1, and we present some estimations of this parameter.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nVESTNIK\nUDMURTSKOGO\nUNIVERSITETA.\nMATEMATIKA.\nMEKH (https://crm.ics.org.ru/uploads/vestnik/3-2024/24-03-05.pdf)\nciteturn7search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; (ii) θ(2[s]) = 3 for odd s ≥3. ... Proof of a local antimagic conjecture, Discrete Mathematics and Theoretical Computer ... On local antimagic chromatic number of cycle-related\n■\n§ 4. Conjecture and Open Problem\nWe have completely characterized s-bridge graphs θs with χla(θs) = 2. We note that the only\nother known resu